    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d334">

                <pb facs="0421" n="407" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg407"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/15358: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-04-28T15:00:00+07:00">April 28, 1944—3
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received April 28—9:28 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>741. At weekly press conference April 26 Government spokesman communicated to
                    correspondents message from Generalissimo, substance <pb facs="0422" n="408"
                        xml:id="pg408"/> of which is as follows: as the German [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Central</hi>] Government has accepted your petition for
                    the trip to the Communist areas, you will certainly be permitted to go. The
                    date, however, will be decided by the Government according to plan and you will
                    be informed when the arrangements have been completed. You are requested not to
                    press the matter. The date cannot be settled by letters or cables from the
                    Communists as the responsibility and authority lie with the Central Government,
                    (Embassy’s 698, April 21, 6 p.m.<anchor n="26" xml:id="d334-f1"/>)</p>
                <p>Letters and cables refer to telegram welcoming the correspondents received from
                    Yenan leaders, to telegram sent by correspondents through Military Affairs
                    Commission [to] Mao Tse-tung asking him to guarantee full freedom of movement
                    and investigation during trip in Communist areas and to letter [from] local
                    Communist representative to correspondents promising such freedom. Spokesman had
                    previously said Government desired to obtain such guarantee from Communists
                    before permitting correspondents to leave.</p>
                <p>Lin Pai-hsu, chairman Yenan Border Government, reportedly has not left Yenan.
                    Correspondents are not permitted to send despatches on Communist problem “until
                    they arrive at Yenan”.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
